<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilkhanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ilkhanid, Ilkhanids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ilkhanate was a Mongol state founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a> in the mid-thirteenth century, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and neighbouring lands. Its rulers later converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and under Ghazan carried out major reforms. It has its own profile on the site, was a rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and was one of the successor khanates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilkhanate.jpg" alt="Ilkhanate" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historical_Atlas_of_Iran_-_Plate_No._17_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mongol alliance that had protected Cilicia failed it in the fourteenth century as the Crusader states and the <b>Ilkhanate</b> disintegrated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…thirteenth and early fourteenth centuries, when Mongol guarantees of safe conduct allowed merchants to travel across Eurasia on an unprecedented scale, benefited the khanate through tolls and taxes on goods passing through its territory, although its frequent wars with the Yuan and the <b>Ilkhanate</b> repeatedly disrupted the routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongols made Tabriz the capital of the <b>Ilkhanate</b> in the second half of the thirteenth century, Trebizond became a principal Black Sea outlet for the route from Persia, through which silk, spices and other goods reached Western merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1262 the khans fought the <b>Ilkhanate of Persia</b> over Azerbaijan and the Caucasus, a war that drew Berke into alliance with the Mamluk sultan Baybars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Eastern Georgia remained under the <b>Ilkhanate</b>, which executed Demetrius II in 1289.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Diplomatically, Mamluk relations with the Golden Horde, the <b>Ilkhanate</b>, the Ottomans and European powers such as Venice and Aragon confirmed its role as a critical player in regional politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
